  <!-- ==================== PAGE 2: OBJECTIVE & STRUCTURE ==================== -->
  <h1>1. Objective</h1>
  <p>
    The objective of this practical work is to design, implement, and rigorously test the relational database schema for a <strong>Government Grievance Redressal Portal</strong>. The work covers normalized table creation, primary and foreign key constraint enforcement, sample civic data insertion in referential integrity safe order, multi-table JOIN operations (INNER, LEFT, RIGHT, FULL, and NATURAL JOINs), composite query analysis, and intentional SQL constraint violation testing for practical database debugging.
  </p>

  <h1>2. Database Structure</h1>
  <p>
    The database contains <strong>5 normalized tables</strong>: <code>departments</code>, <code>users</code>, <code>grievances</code>, <code>resolution_logs</code>, and <code>feedbacks</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The relationships are strictly implemented using primary keys and foreign keys to guarantee municipal audit integrity:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>departments &rarr; users</code>: A department employs multiple Grievance Redressal Officers (GROs) (1:N).</li>
    <li><code>departments &rarr; grievances</code>: A department is assigned to handle multiple municipal grievances (1:N).</li>
    <li><code>users &rarr; grievances</code>: A registered citizen submits and tracks multiple grievance tickets (1:N).</li>
    <li><code>grievances &rarr; resolution_logs</code>: A grievance accumulates sequential action logs and remarks during resolution (1:N).</li>
    <li><code>users (GRO) &rarr; resolution_logs</code>: A GRO writes resolution log entries across assigned department grievances (1:N).</li>
    <li><code>grievances &rarr; feedbacks</code>: A resolved grievance receives exactly one citizen satisfaction feedback and appeal rating (1:1).</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="terminal"><span class="prompt">mysql&gt;</span> <span class="cmd">SHOW DATABASES;</span>
+--------------------+
